CAT = "cemapConsumer"; SEG = "CON"
Q = [
("Before recommending long-term investments, an adviser would normally make sure the client first has:",
 "An emergency cash fund", ["A stocks and shares ISA", "A second property", "An interest-only mortgage"],
 "Ready cash for emergencies comes before investing, so the client is not forced to sell investments at a bad time. Protection needs are also usually met before saving."),
("A couple with a repayment mortgage want life cover that matches the falling debt at the lowest cost. The usual choice is:",
 "Decreasing term assurance", ["Level term assurance", "Whole of life assurance policy", "Family income benefit"],
 "Decreasing term cover falls roughly in line with a repayment mortgage balance, so it is cheaper than level cover. Level term suits an interest-only loan, where the debt stays the same."),
("Which policy replaces part of a person's earnings if illness or injury stops them working?",
 "Income protection", ["Critical illness cover", "Level term assurance", "Private medical insurance"],
 "Income protection pays a regular income after a waiting period while the person cannot work. Critical illness pays a lump sum on diagnosis of a listed condition."),
("Critical illness cover pays out:",
 "A lump sum on a listed diagnosis", ["A monthly income while off work sick", "The bills for private hospital treatment", "A lump sum on death within the term"],
 "The policy pays a tax-free lump sum when the insured is diagnosed with a condition defined in the policy, such as many cancers or a heart attack."),
("A defined contribution pension's eventual value mainly depends on:",
 "Contributions and investment growth", ["Final salary and the number of years served", "The member's state pension age", "The employer's profits that year"],
 "In a defined contribution scheme the pot is what has been paid in plus investment returns, so the member bears the investment risk. Final salary schemes promise a benefit based on pay and service."),
("Under automatic enrolment, an employer must:",
 "Enrol eligible staff in a pension", ["Pay part of each employee's mortgage", "Provide life cover for all staff", "Match any personal pension payments"],
 "Employers must enrol eligible jobholders into a qualifying workplace pension and contribute, though staff may opt out. Automatic enrolment is supervised by The Pensions Regulator."),
("Which is the main reason many people distrust financial services firms?",
 "Past mis-selling scandals", ["Low interest rates on loans", "Too many bank branches closing", "Strict data protection rules"],
 "Large-scale mis-selling of endowments, personal pensions and payment protection insurance damaged trust. Rebuilding it is one aim of the Consumer Duty."),
("Someone with no bank account and little access to mainstream credit is said to be:",
 "Financially excluded", ["Credit impaired", "Mortgage trapped", "Financially overextended"],
 "Financial exclusion means being unable to use mainstream products such as a bank account or affordable credit. It often pushes people towards high-cost lenders."),
("Which service gives free, impartial money and pension guidance to the public?",
 "The Money and Pensions Service", ["The Financial Services Compensation Scheme", "The Prudential Regulation Authority", "The Payment Systems Regulator"],
 "The Money and Pensions Service, set up by government, runs MoneyHelper and Pension Wise for free guidance. The FSCS pays compensation when firms fail."),
("Which is usually the first financial priority for a client with credit card debt at a high interest rate and modest savings?",
 "Clear the expensive debt", ["Open a stocks and shares ISA", "Start a whole of life policy", "Buy a buy-to-let property"],
 "Paying off debt that charges more than savings earn gives a certain return. Long-term investing usually waits until expensive debt is cleared and an emergency fund exists."),
("A young family wants an income paid to them each month until the end of the term if a parent dies. The product designed for this is:",
 "Family income benefit", ["Decreasing term assurance", "An investment bond", "A pension annuity"],
 "Family income benefit pays a regular tax-free income from the death to the end of the term, which suits budgeting for children. Decreasing term pays a lump sum."),
("With an interest-only mortgage, the borrower must also have:",
 "A plan to repay the capital", ["Decreasing term assurance", "A shorter term than usual", "A guarantor for the interest"],
 "Monthly payments cover only interest, so the whole loan is still owed at the end. Lenders and advisers must check there is a credible repayment strategy."),
("Equity release is mainly aimed at:",
 "Older homeowners releasing cash", ["First-time buyers with small deposits", "Landlords buying more property", "Tenants saving for a deposit"],
 "Lifetime mortgages and home reversion let older homeowners unlock value from their home without moving. Advising on them needs a further qualification beyond CeMAP."),
("A whole of life policy differs from term assurance because it:",
 "Pays out whenever death occurs", ["Ends at a fixed date chosen at the start", "Pays only on a critical illness", "Needs no premiums after year one"],
 "Whole of life cover lasts for life, so a claim is certain if premiums are maintained. Term assurance pays only if death happens within the term."),
("A stocks and shares ISA is attractive mainly because:",
 "Returns are free of UK tax", ["The capital is guaranteed", "It pays a fixed interest rate", "It can be used as mortgage security"],
 "Income and gains in an ISA are free of UK income tax and capital gains tax. The value still goes up and down with the investments inside it."),
]
